# File Transfer Safety Standards

## Golden Rule
NEVER delete a source file until the destination file has been:
1. Written completely
2. Verified by size match (source bytes == destination bytes)
3. Confirmed readable (no OS-level error on open)

## Transfer Pattern (Always Use This)
```
copy source → destination
verify destination size == source size
if verified:
    log success
    mark source for deletion queue
    delete source
else:
    log error
    delete incomplete destination file
    flag source for retry or manual review
```

## Atomic Operations
- Never partially move a folder — complete all files or roll back
- If a folder transfer fails mid-way, the destination partial folder must be logged and flagged — never silently left incomplete
- Maintain a transfer_state.json in the session temp directory to allow resume after crash or interruption

## Error Handling
- On any file error: log full details (source path, destination path, error type, timestamp), flag the item, skip it, and continue
- Never let a single file error halt the entire transfer
- Maintain a running errors list accessible in the progress UI

## Exclusion Safety
- Before scanning any directory, check against the exclusion list
- Never touch: System Volume Information, $RECYCLE.BIN, Windows, Program Files, Program Files (x86), AppData
- Never follow symlinks or junction points
- Skip files that are currently open/locked by another process (log and flag, do not retry automatically)

## Duplicate Handling
- Duplicate = same filename AND (same EXIF date OR same creation date within 1 second)
- Near-duplicate = same filename, different dates → rename destination as originalname_2.ext, originalname_3.ext etc.
- All true duplicates moved to _DUPLICATES_REVIEW folder at destination root, preserving their source sub-path for reference
- Duplicate log entry must include: both paths, dates compared, reason flagged as duplicate

## Folder Structure Preservation
- The folder is the atomic unit — never split a folder's contents
- The deepest folder containing media files is the unit that moves
- Parent wrapper folders (containing only subfolders, no direct media files) are navigation containers and are not moved as units
- Destination year/month determined by majority year of files inside the folder — if tied, use the oldest file's year
- Multi-year folders (files spanning more than 2 years) are flagged in the final report with their destination noted

## Logging Requirements
Every file operation must log:
- Timestamp
- Operation type (COPY, VERIFY, DELETE, SKIP, FLAG, DUPLICATE)
- Source full path
- Destination full path
- File size
- Result (SUCCESS, FAILURE, SKIPPED)
- Error message if applicable
